{"title":"2017 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 10","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_10&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>In the diagram, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/4/a/b4a455d533200d7dfbd00731fa8bd9e8796fb803.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle PQR$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> is equilateral and is made up of four smaller equilateral triangles.\n</p>\n<hr />\n<p><i>An image is supposed to go here.  You can help us out by creating one and <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=2017_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_10&amp;action=edit\">editing it in</a></span>.  Thanks.</i>\n</p>\n<hr />\n<p>If each of the smaller triangles has a perimeter of 9 cm, what is the perimeter of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/4/a/b4a455d533200d7dfbd00731fa8bd9e8796fb803.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle PQR$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" />?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/a/77a87d441597be2811279eaf51855b4d5f176608.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}\\ 15 \\text{ cm} \\qquad\\textbf{(B)}\\ 9 \\text{ cm} \\qquad\\textbf{(C)}\\ 36 \\text{ cm} \\qquad\\textbf{(D)}\\ 27 \\text{ cm} \\qquad\\textbf{(E)}\\ 18 \\text{ cm}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"521\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_1_.28n_as_largest.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1_(n_as_largest)\">Solution 1 (n as largest)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_10&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1 (n as largest)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The perimeter of an equilateral triangle is equal to three times its side length. This means that the side length of each of the small equilateral triangles is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/2/2/722527cb0b80fa55ea8bf249c277d91ab45dbe25.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{9}{3} = 3 \\text{ cm}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"75\" height=\"38\" />. Additionally, two equilateral triangles make up the side length of the large equilateral triangle, so the side length of the large equilateral triangle is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/8/6a8c528c8f69e133d517b59b70f100c40d9a77fb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3 \\times 2 = 6 \\text{ cm}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"102\" height=\"15\" />.\n</p><p>The perimeter of the large equilateral triangle is then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/8/b/08b5c8b82ff0bfd5cbe18d8c441282ab1d4d0588.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6 \\times 3 = \\boxed{\\textbf {(E) } 18 \\text{ cm}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"156\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p><p>~anabel.disher\n</p>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)\" title=\"2017 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7)\">2017 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7)</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2017 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems (page does not exist)\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Answer_Key&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2017 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Answer Key (page does not exist)\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://cemc.math.uwaterloo.ca/contests/past_contests.html\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_8\" title=\"2017 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 8\">Problem 8</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">Problem 10</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2017 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2017 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_3\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2017 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2017 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2017 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"2017 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 6\">6</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_7\" title=\"2017 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 7\">7</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_8\" title=\"2017 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 8\">8</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_9\" title=\"2017 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 9\">9</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflin